course: biology
lesson: 124
title: The Lion King: real lions, prides and the circle of life
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In The Lion King, Simba stands on Pride Rock, Mufasa roars across the plain, and the lionesses go hunting; much of this is truer than you might think.
explain: Real Serengeti lions rest on rocky islands called kopjes, which give shade, a lookout and safe dens, and the film makers went to East Africa to study them.
explain: A pride is a family of related lionesses, mothers, sisters, daughters and aunts, who hunt as a team and share the care of all the cubs, while the males guard the land and hunt big prey too.
explain: Young males like Simba really do leave home at two or three years old, and Mufasa's circle of life is real science: lions eat antelopes, antelopes eat grass, and dead lions feed the soil that grows the grass.
explain: But there is no elephant graveyard, and lions are not kings of a happy kingdom: about 20,000 to 25,000 wild lions are left, fewer than half of cubs live to two, and they rest up to 20 hours a day.
fact: Serengeti lions rest on rocky outcrops called kopjes, which give them shade, a lookout and safe dens for cubs.
fact: Lionesses do most of the hunting, often as a team, but males hunt too, especially big animals like buffalo.
fact: A lion's roar can be heard about 8 kilometres away; lions roar to claim their land and keep in touch, and a cub only starts real roaring at about one year old.
fact: Young males leave or are pushed out of the pride at about two or three years old, because the grown males see them as rivals, often wandering with brothers or cousins in a group called a coalition.
fact: Lions sleep or rest up to 20 hours a day and are most active at night and dawn; one lion can eat about 40 kilograms of meat in a meal and then may not eat again for several days.
fact: The great migration is real: over a million wildebeest, with zebras and gazelles, move round the Serengeti and Masai Mara every year following rain and fresh grass.
fact: Cubs play constantly, chasing tails and wrestling, and play is practice for hunting; the black tuft on a lion's tail is unique among cats.
fact: The lionesses of a pride are usually all related and share cub care, and a lioness may let another's cub drink her milk.
fact: There is no real elephant graveyard; elephants die wherever they are, though bones may collect near water where weak old elephants stayed.
fact: Lions in a few places, such as parts of Uganda and Tanzania, often climb trees, and scientists are not sure why they do it there and hardly anywhere else.
fact: The circle of life matches real science: energy passes from grass to antelopes to lions, and decomposers return dead bodies to the soil.
fact: Fewer than half of wild lion cubs survive their first two years.
fact: Wild lions now live mostly in Africa south of the Sahara, plus a small group of Asiatic lions in the Gir Forest in India; about 20,000 to 25,000 are left.
exercise: What are the rocky outcrops that real Serengeti lions rest on called? | Kopjes | Burrows | Badger setts | Squirrel dreys
exercise: Who does most of the hunting in a lion pride? | The lionesses, often as a team | The cubs | The oldest male only | Nobody; lions eat only what others kill
exercise: Do male lions ever hunt? | Yes, especially big animals like buffalo | No, never in their lives, the lionesses feed them | Only when they are cubs | Only fish in rivers
exercise: About how far away can a lion's roar be heard? | About 8 kilometres | Only about 80 metres | About 80 kilometres | Only about 8 metres
exercise: At what age do young male lions usually leave the pride? | About two or three years old | About two or three months old | About twenty years old | They never leave
exercise: What is a group of young male lions wandering together called? | A coalition | A herd | A colony | A swarm
exercise: How long can lions sleep or rest each day? | Up to about 20 hours | Only about 2 hours a day | About 8 hours, like people | They never sleep at all
exercise: Why do huge herds of wildebeest travel round the Serengeti every year? | They follow the rain and the fresh grass | They are running away from one very hungry old lion | They want to see the sea | They go to a winter den
exercise: Why do lion cubs pounce on their mother's tail? | Play is practice for hunting | They are trying to eat it | They are angry with her | To keep her awake
exercise: Who looks after the cubs in a real pride? | The related lionesses | Only the big father lion | Only the mother of each cub | The cubs look after themselves
exercise: Is there a real elephant graveyard where elephants go to die? | No, elephants die wherever they are | Yes, in a valley in Kenya | Yes, in the middle of every desert | Yes, every herd has its own
exercise: Do lions ever climb trees? | Yes, in a few places they often rest in branches | No, lions are far too heavy to get up off the ground | Only cubs, never adults | Only when it rains
exercise: How does Mufasa's circle of life match real science? | Lions eat antelopes, antelopes eat grass, and dead lions feed the soil | Lions eat grass when all the antelopes have run away to the hills | The sun eats the lions at night | Antelopes eat the old lions when they grow too weak to run away from them
exercise: Where do wild lions live today? | Mostly Africa south of the Sahara, plus a few in India | All over Europe and Asia, in forests and on mountains | Only in zoos and safari parks, none in the wild | Only in South America
exercise: About how many wild lions are left? | About 20,000 to 25,000 | About 2 million | About 200 | About 500,000
exercise: What fraction of wild lion cubs survive their first two years? | Fewer than half | Nearly all of them | Exactly all but one | None of them at all
test: «lions are lazy, the girls do all the work» What is right? | Lionesses do most hunting, but males hunt too and guard the land | Right, males never do anything except sleep under trees all day long | Right, males only sleep and eat | Wrong, the cubs do all the hunting
test: «pride of 5 lionesses and 2 lions, how many tail tufts» How many? | 7 | 5 | 2 | 14
test: «3 lions n 4 cubs, how many legs, go» How many? | 28 | 14 | 21 | 24
test: What would happen if a young male stayed in his pride forever? | He would compete with the grown males for the pride | He would turn into a lioness and look after the cubs | Nothing, all males stay | The pride would leave him
test: «Simba should roar at 6 months like in the film» What is right? | A cub mews and grunts and starts real roaring at about one year | Cubs roar loudly from the very day they are born, just like their dads | Lions never roar at all | Only lionesses roar
test: «there's a secret place elephants walk to when they're dying, my uncle said» What is right? | No, they die wherever they are; bones may collect near water | Yes, deep in the jungle, where the old elephants walk alone | Yes, but no one has found it | Yes, every elephant knows the way there from birth, even the calves
test: «lion just ate 40 kilos of zebra, will it eat again tomorrow?» What is likely? | It may go days without eating again | It will be starving in an hour | It will eat another 40 kilos at night | Lions eat ten times a day
test: «why do lions need loads of cubs anyway?» What is the best answer? | Fewer than half of wild cubs survive their first two years | Cubs do the hunting while the grown-ups rest in the shade | Cubs keep the whole pride warm at night by sleeping on top of the adults | Every cub becomes a king
test: «lion asleep up a tree in that film, is it fake» What is right? | It may be real; lions in parts of Uganda and Tanzania often climb trees | Fake, lions cannot climb | Fake, only leopards go up trees, as lions are far too big and heavy | Real, all lions sleep in trees
test: A lioness lets another lioness's cub drink her milk. Why might this help her? | The pride is related, so helping the cub helps her own family | She mistook the cub for a baby wildebeest that had got lost | She has no cubs of her own ever | Lionesses swap their cubs with each other every single morning
test: If lions disappeared from a savanna, what might happen to the grass? | Antelopes could grow in number and eat much more of it | Nothing would change at all | The grass would turn into thick trees and bushes within a year | The grass would stop needing rain
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the division of hunting within a pride of lions. | The lionesses undertake the greater part of the hunting, although males also hunt, particularly large prey | The males undertake the entirety of the hunting | The cubs undertake the greater part of the hunting | No member of the pride hunts, all prey being obtained by theft from hyenas and wild dogs
exam: Identify the principal reason for the annual movement of the wildebeest of the Serengeti. | The herds pursue the rainfall and the fresh grass which follows it | The herds seek to evade a single predator which pursues them throughout the year | The herds proceed towards the coast for the purpose of bathing | The herds retire to subterranean dens for the winter season
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the so-called elephant graveyard? | No such place exists; elephants die wherever they happen to be, though remains may accumulate near water | It is a single valley to which all elderly elephants proceed | It is a location known to every herd and carefully concealed from people and from scientists | It is situated beneath every desert in Africa
exam: Ascertain the approximate number of lions remaining in the wild. | Approximately twenty to twenty-five thousand | Approximately two million, spread across all of Africa | Approximately two hundred | Approximately five hundred thousand
